\chapter{Ownership across an embedding boundary}
Language runtimes manipulate values whose lifetimes differ from the host's
stack frames. A returned function may still need variables from a call that
has already finished. A parser may retain source text for diagnostics. A native
callback may close over host-owned state.

\section{Values and handles}
Choose explicitly between copying a value and retaining a handle. MuDSL AST
nodes and terms are structural values. Directed graphs expose graph-local
handles so mutations can preserve identity. Handles from different graphs
must not be mixed even when their integer representations happen to match.

MuLL lists and records are immutable structural values. Binding reassignment
replaces the value; it does not edit a previously copied list. Native function
values, by contrast, may carry shared state such as a print sink. Copying such
a function preserves that stateful capability.

\section{Runtime lifetime}
The C++ MuLL runtime owns an arena of environments. This keeps captured cells
stable without reference-count cycles between closures and their scopes.
Frames are released when the runtime is destroyed, and \api{maxFrames} bounds
their cumulative number. D uses garbage-collected environments but applies the
same frame-allocation limit for predictable cross-runtime behavior.

Keep a C++ runtime alive while calling any script functions obtained from it.
The runtime is neither copyable nor movable because callbacks refer to its
identity. Create independent instances for independent sessions. This also
provides a natural boundary for package caches and output configuration.
